<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Bakr ibn Sa'd</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1226">1226</a>–<a href="/years/1260">1260</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Bakr ibn Sa'd was atabeg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salghurids">Salghurid dynasty</a> from 1226 to 1260. By submitting to the Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> Ögedei and paying tribute, he saved Fars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> from destruction. The poet Saadi, whose pen name honoured the Salghurids, dedicated the Bustan (1257) and the Gulistan (1258) to the dynasty during his reign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-ibn-sad.jpg" alt="Abu Bakr ibn Sa'd" width="301" height="493" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1604, Mughal artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abu_Bakr_b._Sa%60d_b._Zangi_Folio_from_the_Manuscript_of_Kulliyat_(Collected_Works)_of_Sa%60di_(d._1292)_Accession_Number_AKM284.15.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
